---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.svg";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="Three shelves of bottles, cans and cask-wine boxes reduced to flat gold and black silhouettes, with small price tags below them, in a two-ink risograph print"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    You will treat the bottle shop as a dataset, and you will do so with
    citations. Each week pairs a chemical, fiscal, or spatial question —
    what a sugar wash actually costs to ferment, how the Wine
    Equalisation Tax's ad valorem structure rewards cheap cask wine over
    everything else, why an endcap is stocked the way it is — with the
    peer-reviewed literature that has already measured it. You will
    scrape and audit real supermarket pricing data to calculate the exact
    alcohol-by-volume-to-price ratio for more than a hundred products.
    You will trace a specific tax mechanism's economic and spatial
    consequences in a fifteen-hundred-word policy brief. You will end the
    semester designing the mathematically optimal, cheapest possible
    alcohol inventory for a fifty-person event, pitched with a budget
    spreadsheet that has to survive scrutiny. The artefact you leave with
    is not a shopping list; it is an index you can defend.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    This course is for anyone who has stood in a bottle shop aisle doing
    rough unit-price arithmetic in their head and suspected there was a
    real answer underneath it. It assumes no economics, no data science,
    and no tax law — only a willingness to treat a price tag as a claim
    that can be checked. It is not for anyone hoping for irony: the
    premise is absurd, the method is not, and the two are never allowed
    to acknowledge each other.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly schedule and what happens in each teaching session.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
